    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nSince Python 3.13, the compiler strips the common leading indentation from docstrings (gh-81283). Cython keeps the indentation, so a compiled module's `__doc__` differs from what CPython 3.13+ returns for the same source.\n\nThis shows up for code that reads `__doc__` directly. For example, LangChain's `@tool` uses the docstring as the tool description, so compiling the module changes the text that gets sent to the model.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```python\n# m.py\ndef a():\n    \"\"\"First line.\n\n    Body indented.\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\nCompile it with `cythonize(..., compiler_directives={\"language_level\": \"3\", \"binding\": True})` and compare with the uncompiled module:\n\n```\nCPython 3.13 (m.py):                 'First line.\\n\\nBody indented.\\n'\nCython 3.2.9 / master:               'First line.\\n\\n    Body indented.\\n    '\nCython with embedsignature=True:     'a()\\n\\nFirst line.\\n\\nBody indented.'\n```\n\nThe `embedsignature` variant goes through `inspect.cleandoc()` (#6241), which also drops the trailing newline, so neither variant matches CPython.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\n`__doc__` matches CPython 3.13+, i.e. the same cleanup as `_PyCompile_CleanDoc()`: expand tabs, strip leading spaces from the first line, remove the common margin from the following lines, and keep leading/trailing blank lines and the trailing newline (this differs from `inspect.cleandoc()`).\n\n### OS\n\nmacOS (arm64)\n\n### Python version\n\n3.13.15 (3.12.14 for comparison)\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.2.9 and master (a76568d)\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe difference is already known in the test suite: 6e061108d adjusted `tests/run/r_docstrings.pyx` with `.strip()` on the CPython side, but the compiled side still keeps the indentation. I couldn't find an existing issue for it.\n\nI have a small parser-level patch that does this cleanup when docstrings are parsed (leaving `cdef extern` verbatim code alone), and it matches CPython 3.13 in my tests. Before opening a PR, I'd like to ask which behaviour you'd prefer:\n\n1. always dedent, regardless of the Python version at runtime (simplest; changes `__doc__` on Python <= 3.12, much like #6241 did for embedsignature, see #6904)\n2. dedent only for Python 3.13+ (needs version-dependent docstring constants, and can't be exact for Limited API builds)\n3. put it behind a directive\n",
